  <section>
    <p class="post-lead"><strong>Short answer:</strong> a hacked Telegram account doesn't erase your Stars balance or Premium subscription — it hands control of them to whoever broke in. They can spend your Stars, gift them to another account, or send fake Premium-gift links to your contacts, and none of it can be undone. This falls outside any reseller's delivery guarantee, because the failure isn't delivery — it's account security. The fix that actually works is prevention: Two-Step Verification (2FA), set before anything happens.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What actually happens when the account is taken over</h2>
    <p>Once someone controls your Telegram account, Telegram treats every action they take as coming from you — there's no separate "hacked" state that blocks spending.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The Stars balance gets spent.</strong> Settings → Stars shows the balance in plain view. It can be gifted to another account the attacker controls, or spent on a paid post or bot, in seconds.</li>
      <li><strong>Premium gets used to scam your contacts.</strong> A common pattern: a message goes out to your entire contact list claiming "I gifted you a year of Premium, click here." Anyone who clicks loses their own account the same way — that's how it spreads.</li>
      <li><strong>Any linked payment method is exposed.</strong> If a card or auto-renew is attached to the account, the attacker can buy more Stars or Premium and move it out before you notice.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>This is a different problem from <a href="/en/blog/is-buying-telegram-stars-safe">being scammed by a fake reselling bot</a>. There, the mistake is picking the wrong service. Here, the account itself has been taken — the service you bought from is irrelevant.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>How accounts actually get taken over</h2>
    <p>Almost none of this happens by guessing a password. It happens because someone gets the SMS login code.</p>
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Method</th><th>How it works</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Phishing page</td><td>A fake site styled like Telegram's login screen. Whatever code you type there goes straight to the attacker.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>"Forward me the code" trick</td><td>A message, often from a compromised contact, says "I got a code by mistake, can you forward it" — it's actually your own login code.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SIM swap</td><td>The attacker convinces a mobile carrier to move your number to their SIM, so the SMS code arrives on their phone directly.</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Session hijacking</td><td>Malware or an unattended Telegram Web session lets an attacker lift an active session token without ever seeing a code.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p>If codes simply aren't arriving at all — a different, more common problem — see <a href="/en/blog/telegram-sms-code-not-arriving">Telegram SMS code not arriving</a>.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What to do in the first few minutes</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Terminate every other session.</strong> If you can still log in from any device, go to <strong>Settings → Devices</strong> and end all other sessions immediately. This removes the attacker on the spot.</li>
      <li><strong>Set a new Two-Step password right away.</strong> Settings → Privacy and Security → Two-Step Verification.</li>
      <li><strong>Check Stars and payment activity.</strong> Settings → Stars — an unexpected gift sent out or a purchase you didn't make is the clearest sign something happened.</li>
      <li><strong>Warn your contacts.</strong> If the account was compromised even briefly, a fake message may already be sitting in dozens of chats. Tell people not to click it.</li>
      <li><strong>Contact Telegram support if you're locked out.</strong> On the "Log in with SMS" screen, use "Report a login problem," or message <strong>@SpamBot</strong> directly — that's Telegram's own support channel.</li>
    </ol>
    <p><strong>One detail worth knowing:</strong> if you never set a Two-Step password, Telegram's own policy reopens the account to you automatically 7 days after a new device logs in, even without support intervening. With a 2FA password already set, that window can extend — which is exactly why setting it in advance matters.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Can stolen Stars or Premium be recovered?</h2>
    <p>Realistically, no. <a href="/en/blog/telegram-stars-or-premium">Telegram Stars purchases are final</a> by design, and account compromise doesn't change that — from the system's point of view, the spend came from "you." The same applies to Premium activated or gifted away under your name.</p>
    <p>This is also why a reseller's 100% guarantee — including StarsJoy's — applies specifically to an order that <strong>never got delivered</strong> (see <a href="/en/blog/is-starsjoy-legit">is StarsJoy legit</a>). If Stars or Premium arrived successfully and the account was compromised afterward through phishing or a SIM swap, that's outside what any delivery guarantee can cover — the failure happened after delivery, not during it.</p>
    <p>Which is the whole argument for prevention over recovery: there is close to no recovery path once it's gone.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Setting up Two-Step Verification</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Open Telegram → <strong>Settings → Privacy and Security</strong></li>
      <li>Tap <strong>Two-Step Verification</strong></li>
      <li>Set a password you don't reuse anywhere else</li>
      <li>Add a recovery email — you'll need it if you ever forget the password</li>
    </ol>
    <p>With this on, an attacker holding only your SMS code still can't get in — they need the password too. It's free, takes under a minute, and blocks the method behind most account takeovers.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Does buying Stars or Premium through a reseller put the account at risk?</h2>
    <p>Not when the reseller only asks for a username. <a href="https://starsjoy.uz/en" target="_blank">StarsJoy</a> works as a Telegram Mini App: delivery uses Telegram's own gift mechanism, triggered by nothing more than the recipient's public username. No password, SMS code, or account access is ever part of the transaction — so a purchase itself carries no exposure. The risk described in this article comes entirely from phishing, SIM swaps and session theft, not from buying Stars.</p>
    <p>For the warning signs that do apply when choosing where to buy, see <a href="/en/blog/is-buying-telegram-stars-safe">is buying Telegram Stars safe</a>.</p>
  </section>

